Here's a C# program that generates all possible palindromic anagrams of a given word:
```
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Console.Write("Enter a word: ");
        string word = Console.ReadLine();
        var anagrams = GenerateAnagrams(word).Where(IsPalindrome);
        Console.WriteLine("Palindromic Anagrams:");
        foreach (var anagram in anagrams)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(anagram);
        }
        Console.ReadKey();
    }
    static IEnumerable<string> GenerateAnagrams(string word)
    {
        if (word.Length == 1)
        {
            yield return word;
        }
        else
        {
            foreach (var letter in word)
            {
                var remainingLetters = word.Replace(letter.ToString(), "");
                foreach (var anagram in GenerateAnagrams(remainingLetters))
                {
                    yield return letter + anagram;
                }
            }
        }
    }
    static bool IsPalindrome(string word)
    {
        return word == new string(word.Reverse().ToArray());
    }
}
```
To generate all possible anagrams of a word, we use a recursive function called `GenerateAnagrams`. This function takes a word as input and returns all possible anagrams of that word by recursively generating anagrams of the remaining letters and concatenating them with the current letter. The base case is when the word has only one letter, in which case we return that letter as the only anagram.
To check if a string is a palindrome, we use the `IsPalindrome` function, which simply checks if the string is the same forwards and backwards.
Finally, we use LINQ to filter the anagrams to only those that are palindromes, and then output the results to the console.

