There are several approaches to signal processing in MATLAB for EEG signals, including filtering, artifact rejection, feature extraction, and classification. Here is an example code snippet to perform basic filtering and artifact rejection analysis using the eeg_data.mat file:
```matlab
% Load the EEG data
load('eeg_data.mat');
% Define the sampling rate
fs = 500;
% Define the filter parameters
f1 = 1; % low cutoff frequency (Hz)
f2 = 50; % high cutoff frequency (Hz)
order = 4; % filter order
% Design the filter
[b,a] = butter(order, [f1/(fs/2) f2/(fs/2)]);
% Apply the filter to the EEG data
eeg_filtered = filtfilt(b,a,eeg_data);
% Perform artifact rejection
threshold = 100; % threshold for artifact rejection (uV)
eeg_cleaned = eeg_filtered;
for i = 1:size(eeg_cleaned,2)
    channel = eeg_cleaned(:,i);
    channel_std = std(channel);
    channel_mean = mean(channel);
    channel(channel > channel_mean + threshold*channel_std) = NaN;
    channel(channel < channel_mean - threshold*channel_std) = NaN;
    eeg_cleaned(:,i) = channel;
end
% Plot the original and cleaned EEG data
t = (0:length(eeg_data)-1)/fs;
figure;
subplot(2,1,1);
plot(t,eeg_data);
title('Original EEG Data');
xlabel('Time (s)');
ylabel('Amplitude (uV)');
subplot(2,1,2);
plot(t,eeg_cleaned);
title('Cleaned EEG Data');
xlabel('Time (s)');
ylabel('Amplitude (uV)');
```
This code snippet loads the eeg_data.mat file and defines the sampling rate as 500 Hz. It then designs a fourth-order Butterworth filter with a low cutoff frequency of 1 Hz and a high cutoff frequency of 50 Hz. The filter is applied to the EEG data using the `filtfilt` function, which performs zero-phase filtering. 
Next, the code performs artifact rejection by setting any data points that are more than 100 uV away from the mean to NaN. This is done for each channel in the EEG data. 
Finally, the original and cleaned EEG data are plotted using the `plot` function. The original EEG data is plotted in the top subplot, and the cleaned EEG data is plotted in the bottom subplot. 
Note that this is a basic example of filtering and artifact rejection, and more advanced techniques may be necessary for more complex analyses.

