Imagine a Java program is like a superhero with superpowers. The more powerful the superhero, the greater the responsibility they have to use their powers for good. Similarly, in a Java program, the more capabilities and functionalities it has, the greater the responsibility of the programmer to ensure that it is used ethically and responsibly.
To exemplify this concept, consider the following code snippet in Java:
```
public class SuperHero {
    private String name;
    private int powerLevel;
    public SuperHero(String name, int powerLevel) {
        this.name = name;
        this.powerLevel = powerLevel;
    }
    public void usePower() {
        if (powerLevel > 10) {
            System.out.println(name + " used their powers for good!");
        } else {
            System.out.println(name + " used their powers for evil...");
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, the `SuperHero` class represents a powerful entity, like a Java program. The `powerLevel` variable represents the level of power or functionality of the program. The `usePower()` method represents the actions that the program can take.
Notice how the method includes a check to ensure that if the power level is above a certain threshold (10), the program will use its powers for good. This is an example of the responsibility that comes with having a powerful program - the programmer must ensure that it is used for the right purposes.
So, to sum it up, just like a superhero with great power has the responsibility to use their powers for good, a Java program with great functionality has the responsibility to be used ethically and responsibly.

