How can I generate a random indoor sport from a list of options in Go, while making sure that the same sport is not selected twice in a row? Also, can you provide code to keep track of the number of times each sport has been selected and print the statistics after 10 selections?
Here's a sample code that does what you need:
package main
import (
	"fmt"
	"math/rand"
	"time"
)
func main() {
	sports := []string{"basketball", "volleyball", "badminton", "table tennis", "squash", "racquetball"}
	rand.Seed(time.Now().UnixNano())
	selectedSports := make([]string, 0)
	stats := make(map[string]int)
	for i := 0; i < 10; i++ {
		var selectedSport string
		if i == 0 {
			selectedSport = sports[rand.Intn(len(sports))]
		} else {
			for {
				temp := sports[rand.Intn(len(sports))]
				if temp != selectedSports[i-1] {
					selectedSport = temp
					break
				}
			}
		}
		selectedSports = append(selectedSports, selectedSport)
		stats[selectedSport]++
		fmt.Printf("Selection #%d: %s\n", i+1, selectedSport)
	}
	fmt.Println("\nSelection stats:")
	for _, sport := range sports {
		fmt.Printf("%s: %d\n", sport, stats[sport])
	}
}
This code uses the rand package to generate a random number and the time package to seed the random number generator. It then uses a loop to make 10 selections from the list of sports.
If it's the first selection, it just selects a random sport. Otherwise, it keeps generating random sports until it gets one that is different from the previous selection.
After each selection, it adds the selected sport to a slice and updates a map that keeps track of the number of times each sport has been selected.
Finally, it prints out the selection stats.

