To encrypt the message, you can use the RSA encryption algorithm. The RSA algorithm uses a public key to encrypt the message and a private key to decrypt it. Here is an example of how to use the RSA algorithm in Swift:
```
import Security
func encryptMessage(message: String, publicKey: SecKey) -> Data? {
    let messageData = message.data(using: .utf8)!
    var error: Unmanaged<CFError>?
    guard let encryptedData = SecKeyCreateEncryptedData(publicKey, .rsaEncryptionOAEPSHA256, messageData as CFData, &error) as Data? else {
        print("Error encrypting message: \(error!.takeRetainedValue() as Error)")
        return nil
    }
    return encryptedData
}
func decryptMessage(encryptedMessage: Data, privateKey: SecKey) -> String? {
    var error: Unmanaged<CFError>?
    guard let decryptedData = SecKeyCreateDecryptedData(privateKey, .rsaEncryptionOAEPSHA256, encryptedMessage as CFData, &error) as Data? else {
        print("Error decrypting message: \(error!.takeRetainedValue() as Error)")
        return nil
    }
    return String(data: decryptedData, encoding: .utf8)
}
```
To use this code, you would need to generate a public-private key pair and share the public key with the intended recipient. The recipient would then use their private key to decrypt the message.
