To calculate the area of a shape made up of multiple squares, we can break it down into smaller squares and add up their areas. Here's some sample C# code to do this:
```
int numSquares = 10; // replace with the actual number of squares
int area = numSquares * numSquares; // each square has an area of 1 unit, so we can simply multiply the number of squares by itself
Console.WriteLine("The area of the shape is " + area + " square units.");
```
To calculate the area of a triangle made up of squares with side lengths of 1 unit, we can arrange the squares to form a right triangle. Here's some sample C# code to do this:
```
int baseLength = 5; // replace with the actual length of the base of the triangle in number of squares
int height = 3; // replace with the actual height of the triangle in number of squares
int area = (baseLength * height) / 2; // the area of a triangle is half the product of its base and height
Console.WriteLine("The area of the triangle is " + area + " square units.");
```
To extend the code to calculate the area of a circle with a radius of 1 unit, we can use the squares to approximate the shape of the circle. Here's some sample C# code to do this:
```
double radius = 1; // replace with the actual radius of the circle
int numSquares = 1000; // increase this number to get a more accurate approximation
int numSquaresInsideCircle = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < numSquares; i++)
{
    double x = (double)i / numSquares;
    for (int j = 0; j < numSquares; j++)
    {
        double y = (double)j / numSquares;
        if (Math.Sqrt((x - 0.5) * (x - 0.5) + (y - 0.5) * (y - 0.5)) <= radius)
        {
            numSquaresInsideCircle++;
        }
    }
}
double area = (double)numSquaresInsideCircle / (numSquares * numSquares) * 4 * radius * radius; // the area of a circle is pi times the square of its radius
Console.WriteLine("The area of the circle is " + area + " square units.");
```
Note that this code uses a Monte Carlo method to approximate the area of the circle. It generates a large number of points inside a square and counts how many of them are also inside the circle. The ratio of the number of points inside the circle to the total number of points is an estimate of the ratio of the area of the circle to the area of the square. Multiplying this ratio by the area of the square gives an estimate of the area of the circle.

