Here's a sample JavaScript code snippet that uses regular expressions to remove stop words:
const sentence = "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog";
const stopWords = ['the', 'a', 'an', 'and', 'of', 'to', 'in', 'that', 'is', 'for'];
const filteredSentence = sentence.replace(/\b\w+\b/g, function(word) {
  if (stopWords.indexOf(word.toLowerCase()) === -1) {
    return word;
  } else {
    return '';
  }
}).trim();
console.log(filteredSentence);
This code replaces any word in the input sentence that appears in a predefined list of stop words with an empty string. The resulting filtered sentence is then printed to the console.

