Dynamic programming is important because it can significantly reduce the time complexity of a problem by avoiding redundant computations. A naive recursive algorithm may repeatedly compute the same subproblems, leading to an exponential increase in the number of computations required to solve the problem. Dynamic programming, on the other hand, stores the results of subproblems and reuses them as needed, resulting in a much more efficient algorithm.
Here is an example of the Fibonacci sequence implemented using both a naive recursive algorithm and dynamic programming:
Naive recursive algorithm:
```
int fibonacci(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) {
        return n;
    }
    return fibonacci(n-1) + fibonacci(n-2);
}
```
Dynamic programming:
```
int fibonacci(int n) {
    int dp[n+1];
    dp[0] = 0;
    dp[1] = 1;
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        dp[i] = dp[i-1] + dp[i-2];
    }
    return dp[n];
}
```
In the naive recursive algorithm, the function repeatedly computes the same subproblems, leading to an exponential increase in the number of computations required to solve the problem. In contrast, the dynamic programming solution stores the results of subproblems in an array and reuses them as needed, resulting in a much more efficient algorithm with a time complexity of O(n).

